#text(size: 2em)[Misfiring Environmentalism]
#v(-4mm)
#text(size: 1.2em, weight: "bold")[_The Economist_, 01/07/2023]

#set text(font: "EB Garamond")

#v(1cm)

// THANK GOODNESS for the enthusiasts and the obsessives. If everyone always took a balanced view of everything, nothing would ever get done. But when campaigners' worldview *seeps* into the *staid* apparatus of policymaking and global forums, bad decisions tend to follow. That, unfortunately, is especially true in the world of climate change.

// One example is the effect of global warming on the world's poorest people. As the planet heats up, extreme events such as *droughts*, floods and storms are becoming more common and more severe. Many places are becoming less habitable. Over the coming decades many vulnerable farmers, from Mali to the Mekong Delta, will find their *crops* failing more frequently. And as resources grow scarcer, more fighting will break out.

// This pattern is no longer just a warning by activists. It is accepted by the mainstream to the point where fears of a surge in climate migration are fodder for the nativist right. Because people are understandably troubled by the idea of climate change forcing poor farmers to leave behind their ancestral lands, an important goal of adaptation spending is to help them stay.

// Yet the truth is more complex. The vast majority of displaced people will not cross international borders but move within their own country. By 2050, 50m–216m people could be on the move internally. And many will be rural folk moving to cities, where lives are likely to become better. Urbanisation usually aids development, bringing people closer to schools, health care and paying jobs, as well as more liberal social norms, particularly for women. This is not an argument in favour of climate change; it suggests that one cost-effective and beneficial form of climate-adaptation spending would be helping people move, rather than preserving small farms in ever-harsher conditions.

// There is another, more profound, example of the danger of climate groupthink. From the panels of Davos to the pages of newspapers, it is increasingly argued that no *trade-off* exists between the economic development of low- and middle-income countries and reducing their greenhouse-gas emissions. This is partly because much of the rich world has successfully made cuts in emissions while continuing to grow, and its leaders want more of the same. But more crucially, it is because governments and development banks with limited budgets struggle to admit that not all their goals can be reconciled, and that they must therefore choose between them.

// Yet choose they must, because the trade-off is in plain sight. Growth is the best way to lift people out of poverty and improve average living standards. But in the developing world, more growth still leads to more emissions. Researchers at the IMF have found that in 72 developing countries since 1990, a 1% rise in annual GDP was on average associated with a 0.7% rise in emissions. By 2030, fast-growing India and Indonesia alone will have increased their annual emissions by the equivalent of over 500m tonnes of carbon dioxide—an extra Germany's-worth of greenhouse-gas belching. In other big emerging markets such as Brazil, Egypt and the Philippines, emissions are rising, too.

// Many rich-world leaders say they can square the circle by funding green development projects which, in theory, cut emissions and boost growth at the same time. That is true to a degree. But, without adequate carbon pricing and cross-border emissions trading to encourage the private sector to invest on its own initiative, it is an enormously expensive and fiendishly complex ask. On June 23rd, at the conclusion of a summit in Paris, rich countries again pledged to meet a target of providing \\$100bn a year in "climate finance" to fund such projects. Yet that is only a fraction of the \\$2.8trn annual investment thought to be needed by 2030 to put the developing world on a green growth path, at least \\$1trn of which probably needs to come from rich countries.

// The reality of limited resources worsens the trade-off. The need to spend money decarbonising big developing economies that already offer citizens reasonable services threatens aid budgets which help pay for things like vaccines and schooling in the poorest parts of Africa. Unlike Brazil or India, say, such nations are unlikely ever to contribute significantly to global emissions. They lose out, however, when foreign aid and loans come with green strings attached. As well as facing stingier health-care and education budgets, they might find scant funding for expanding a gas-powered electricity grid, even though nobody stands ready to pay for the far greater costs of converting it to a green one. African governments rightly resent being told to cut emissions rather than help people in desperate need—especially given that Westerners continue to belch carbon.

// As a result, while leaders offer bromides about sustainable growth, an epic fight for resources rages behind the scenes between those who favour development as practised in decades past and those who want the world's foreign-aid apparatus to turn *wholeheartedly* towards decarbonisation. It is a battle over what is worse: a poorer today or a hotter tomorrow.

// That is an excruciating choice, given the moral force of the argument that the rich world should pay the developing world's climate bills. Global temperatures depend on the stock of carbon in the atmosphere, not the current flow of emissions. On a per-person basis, the rich world has been disproportionately responsible for rising global temperatures and has more capacity to respond to them. Poor countries lack the resources to invest to cut emissions or adapt to climate change themselves; yet relative to the size of their economies, they face the biggest costs.

// As with the decision between *forestalling* or accommodating climate-induced migration, pretending that this choice does not exist helps no one. Politics means that neither an adequate carbon price nor sufficient Western money are likely. Limited resources make it essential to squeeze as much value as possible out of what is available. Squeamishness about weighing costs and benefits --- *stemming* from a well-meaning desire to avoid every injustice --- gets in the way. And the consequences of that evasion fall most heavily on those in the greatest need.

THANK GOODNESS for people who care deeply about one issue and push hard for it. If everyone always tried to see every side of every problem, nothing would get done. But when these passionate campaigners' views spread into the serious world of policymaking, bad decisions often follow. This is especially true when it comes to climate change.

One example is how global warming affects the world's poorest people. As the planet heats up, extreme weather like droughts, floods, and storms is becoming more common and more severe. Many places are becoming harder to live in. Over the coming decades, many vulnerable farmers—from Mali to the Mekong Delta—will see their crops fail more often. And as resources become scarcer, more conflict will break out.

This is no longer just something activists warn about—most people now accept it as true. In fact, fears about a rise in climate migration are now used by right-wing nationalist politicians to argue against immigration. Because people are understandably uncomfortable with the idea of climate change forcing poor farmers to abandon their land, a major goal of climate spending is to help these people stay where they are.

But the reality is more complicated. Most people who are displaced will not cross into other countries—they will move within their own country. By 2050, somewhere between 50 million and 216 million people could be on the move inside their home countries. Many will move from rural areas to cities, where their lives will likely improve. Moving to cities usually helps development: it brings people closer to schools, healthcare, and paying jobs, and it often comes with more progressive social attitudes, especially for women. This doesn't mean climate change is a good thing—but it suggests that helping people move to cities could be a more effective use of climate-adaptation money than trying to keep small farms running in increasingly difficult conditions.

There's another, more serious example of dangerous groupthink around climate change. Increasingly, people at forums like Davos and in newspapers argue that there's no trade-off between economic growth in poorer countries and cutting their greenhouse gas emissions. This idea is partly popular because rich countries have managed to cut emissions while still growing their economies, and their leaders want to see that repeated elsewhere. But mainly, it's because governments and development banks with limited money don't want to admit that they can't achieve every goal at once—and that they have to choose between them.

But choosing is unavoidable, because the trade-off clearly exists. Economic growth is the best way to reduce poverty and raise living standards. But in developing countries, more growth still means more emissions. Researchers at the IMF found that across 72 developing countries since 1990, a 1% increase in yearly economic growth was linked, on average, to a 0.7% increase in emissions. By 2030, fast-growing countries like India and Indonesia alone will add emissions equal to an extra Germany's worth of greenhouse gases. Emissions are also rising in other major developing economies like Brazil, Egypt, and the Philippines.

Many leaders in rich countries claim they can solve this problem by funding green development projects that both cut emissions and boost growth at the same time. That's partly true. But without proper carbon pricing and international emissions trading to encourage private companies to invest on their own, this approach is extremely expensive and complicated. At the end of a summit in Paris on June 23rd, rich countries again promised \$100 billion a year in "climate finance" for such projects. But that's only a small fraction of the \$2.8 trillion needed each year by 2030 to put developing countries on a green growth path—at least \$1 trillion of which should come from rich countries.

Limited resources make this trade-off even worse. Spending money to reduce emissions in large developing economies that already provide decent services to their citizens takes away from aid budgets that fund things like vaccines and education in the poorest parts of Africa. Unlike Brazil or India, these poorer nations will likely never contribute much to global emissions. Yet they suffer when foreign aid and loans come with conditions requiring green policies. Along with facing smaller healthcare and education budgets, they may struggle to find funding even to expand basic gas-powered electricity, while no one offers to pay the much higher cost of building a green power grid instead. African governments are right to resent being told to cut emissions instead of receiving help for urgent needs—especially since wealthy countries continue to produce large amounts of carbon themselves.

As a result, while leaders talk publicly about "sustainable growth," a major struggle over money is happening behind the scenes—between those who support traditional development approaches and those who want the global foreign-aid system to focus entirely on cutting emissions. It's a fight over which is worse: staying poor now, or facing a hotter planet later.

This is an incredibly difficult choice, especially given the strong moral argument that rich countries should pay for the developing world's climate costs. Global temperatures depend on the total amount of carbon already in the atmosphere, not just current emissions. Per person, rich countries have contributed far more to rising temperatures and have more resources to deal with the consequences. Poor countries don't have the money to cut their own emissions or adapt to climate change—yet they face the highest costs relative to the size of their economies.

Just like with the choice between preventing or managing climate-driven migration, pretending this trade-off doesn't exist doesn't help anyone. Realistically, neither a fair carbon price nor enough funding from the West is likely to happen. Since resources are limited, it's essential to use them as wisely as possible. Being too uncomfortable to weigh costs and benefits—even though this discomfort comes from a good place, wanting to avoid unfairness—only makes things worse. And the people who suffer most from this avoidance are those who need help the most.

